**Read:** [Sedimentary Geology — John Southard, MIT 12.110](../sources/MIT-Sedimentary-Geology.lingua#r_7FWZZ66CT5G1FP2JPQMWRV1YHM), the complete carbonate sediments/rocks and diagenesis notes; [Rock Classification Scheme — British Geological Survey](../sources/BGS-Rock-Classification-Scheme.lingua#r_0715XKKA34SBMSRTCCZD3KJQEH), the complete applicable carbonate/sedimentary criteria; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full carbonate-mineral sections.

Carbonate rocks combine skeletal and non-skeletal components, mud, cement, pores, and altered/replaced material. Mineralogy, grain/fabric relationships, fossils, structures, and regional context provide evidence of production, transport, deposition, and later change. A carbonate percentage alone cannot determine environment or history.

Use the named textural classification with its actual criteria and uncertainty. Distinguish depositional support/fabric from later recrystallization, dissolution, cementation, dolomitization, compaction, and deformation. An apparently crystalline texture may have obscured much of the original sedimentary evidence.

Carbonate reactions and fluid exchange connect chemistry with texture. Temperature, fluid composition, pressure, redox, biological mediation, kinetics, and openness can influence products. A simple equilibrium calculation or isotopic value needs its reference/model and cannot independently reconstruct every fluid or burial event.

**Work:** complete the carbonate notes and describe a documented section with observed components and inferred alteration stages separated. Build two possible depositional/diagenetic histories and identify a discriminating textural, mineralogical, chemical, or chronological observation. Connect one reaction to an explicit mass/fluid account and explain what was added, removed, or merely redistributed.

Read the entire carbonate and carbonate-diagenesis sequence. Later hydrogeology, cement/materials, and geochemistry studies reuse these minerals and reactions with different system boundaries and practical questions.

**Continue:** [Reconstruct diagenesis through textures, reactions, fluids, and burial constraints](Diagenesis-compaction-cement-and-porosity-history.lingua#r_4RF28CK89JY6YNRYSP4E0PW79Y).

